From Laravel Pint to DressCode
******************************

.[perex]
Laravel Pint formats code with its `laravel` preset, and the `laravel` standard of DressCode has the same style. Migrating takes one line of configuration, and in a project with Pint, `dresscode init` writes it by itself. The same run that formats your code can also upgrade it to a newer PHP and Laravel.


DressCode as an Alternative to Laravel Pint
===========================================

The default preset of Pint, `laravel`, is a list of fixers from PHP CS Fixer, which Pint is built on. The `laravel` standard carries the same list, so it leaves code that Pint formatted almost unchanged. This was verified on fourteen Laravel packages, 1,615 files run through Pint 1.32: `dresscode check --use laravel` found 129 places in them, and each of them is one of the differences described below (October 2026).

What you get in addition:

- **Upgrading in the same run.** The `modernizations` set rewrites code to the constructs of a newer PHP version, `deprecations` fixes what PHP has deprecated, and the `dresscode/upgrade-laravel` package rewrites the API that Laravel changed between versions. See [Upgrading to a Newer PHP |upgrading-php] and [Laravel |upgrading-laravel].
- **Output for AI agents and editors.** The `agent` format tells an AI agent what changed in a file after the agent edited it, so that its next edit does not fail. See [AI agents |ai-agents].

Pint has one advantage DressCode cannot match: it comes with Laravel. Every new Laravel application has it in `require-dev`, so it is there before anyone decides anything, while DressCode is something you [install |getting-started#Installation] yourself.


Converting the Configuration
============================

Which way to go depends on what is in your `pint.json`.

**Without a `pint.json`, or with a preset only,** one line of configuration is enough:

```neon
use: laravel
```

[`dresscode init` |generate-configuration] writes it too: in a project that requires `laravel/pint` or has a `pint.json`, it picks the standard that matches the Pint preset by itself, and it also measures where your code departs from it. For the default `laravel` preset, it prints:

/--pre .[terminal]
Standard   laravel (the style of Laravel Pint), Laravel Pint formats the project in this style
\--

**With extra rules in the `rules` key on top of the preset,** run `dresscode migrate config`. These are rules of PHP CS Fixer, so it translates them together with the preset, carries the `exclude` key over to `excludePaths`, and lists what has no counterpart at the end of the file, see [Migrating to DressCode |migration#migrate-config]. `init` would not carry the rules into the file; it would only compare them with the code. Both ways write `dresscode.neon`, and `migrate config` does not overwrite an existing file, so choose one.

You can also look up the decision for a rule yourself: `dresscode catalogue` lists, for every decision, the PHP CS Fixer fixers it covers, so you will find `no_unused_imports` as `imports.unused`. The page [PHP CS Fixer Rules |php-cs-fixer-rules] has the whole table.

| Pint | DressCode |
|---|---|
| `pint` | `dresscode fix` |
| `pint --test` | `dresscode check` |
| `pint app/Models` | `dresscode fix app/Models` |
| `pint --preset laravel` | `dresscode fix --use laravel` |


Where DressCode Differs from Pint
=================================

On code that Pint formatted, you will run into these differences:

- DressCode shortens and imports a class name in a doc comment in every tag; Pint does it only in the tags its `fully_qualified_strict_types` lists, which means not in `@template` or `@method`.
- DressCode merges two blank lines in a row inside a doc comment into one. Pint trims only its start and end.
- DressCode treats an import whose name appears only in the text of a comment as unused and removes it. Pint keeps it.
- In a comparison of a literal with a constant, `'\\' === DIRECTORY_SEPARATOR`, DressCode puts the constant on the left. Pint leaves both sides as they are.
- Line endings follow the majority in the file (`file.lineEnding: majority`), so a project on Windows with CRLF stays with CRLF. Pint writes LF.

And here is what Pint does and DressCode does not do yet: renaming test methods to snake_case (`php_unit_method_casing`), sorting traits (`ordered_traits`), and aligning or sorting phpDoc tags (`phpdoc_align`, `phpdoc_order`, `phpdoc_separation`). The page [Standards and Sets |php-coding-standards#laravel] shows the whole preset, with comments.


The First Fix
=============

The first `dresscode fix` brings the code in line with the new configuration. Make it a separate commit with no other changes, so that the history shows what the change of tools brought. Then replace `pint` and `pint --test` with `dresscode fix` and `dresscode check` in the scripts of `composer.json`, in CI and in the Git hook (see [Integrations |integrations]); after that, you can remove the `laravel/pint` package from `require-dev`.

